course: dictionary
lesson: 9799
title: Sustain: keep it going
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word sustain has a few meanings, and the clues around it tell you which one you need. In «The Wright brothers made the first powered, controlled, sustained (kept going, not just a hop) flight», the brackets tell you that sustained means kept going for a long time, not just a quick hop.
explain: Food and water sustain life. The clue is the job they do: they keep you alive. So to sustain someone means to give them what they need to live and stay strong.
explain: In the news, people sustain injuries in accidents. Here sustain means suffer, which is a formal word. Ask yourself: what would fit here? You could say «people suffer injuries», so sustain must mean suffer in this sentence.
fact: To sustain something is to keep it going for a long time.
fact: To sustain someone is to give them the food, strength or support they need to live.
fact: To sustain an injury or damage is to suffer it. This is a formal use, often seen in the news.
fact: To sustain a weight is to hold it up without breaking.
fact: «Sustainable» means using resources so they don't run out.
exercise: In «sustained flight», what does sustained mean? | Very short | Made of wood | Painted bright red | *Kept going for a long time
exercise: Food and water sustain life. What does sustain mean here? | Wash away | Sell at a shop | Make something louder | *Keep alive by giving what is needed
exercise: Which word in «the first powered, controlled, sustained flight» shows the flight was kept going, not just a hop? | gliders | 1903 | balloons | *sustained
test: To sustain an injury means what? | To ignore it forever | To heal it at once | *To suffer it | To use it up
test: In sport, a sustained effort means what? | Something made of glass | A single quick try | *Effort kept up for a long time | A loud shout
exam: Which word means using resources so they don't run out? | Sweet | *Sustainable | Sudden | Sticky
